  "knowledgePattern": "Recover a positive geometric ratio from separated surd terms",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "sequences"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>Let the first term be a and common ratio r. All terms are positive, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The two supplied terms give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow></math>. Dividing them yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>18</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p>This is the square of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, which is positive. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The positive square root is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, since its square has the required value. It lies between zero and one, so the sum to infinity exists.</p><p>Now <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt></mrow></math> after rationalising. Answer G.</p>",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Write the term equations",
      "body": "<p>Call the first term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> and the common ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>. Write expressions for the third and seventh terms, then divide to eliminate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "The nth GP term has ratio power n minus 1.",
      "pitfall": "Every term is positive, so the common ratio is positive.",
      "pause": "Find an equation involving only r.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l09",
          "reminder": "The nth GP term has ratio power n minus 1."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Look for a squared surd",
      "body": "<p>Rationalise your expression. When taking square roots, try writing the surd numerator as a square of the form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>v</mi><msqrt><mn>5</mn></msqrt><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Expanding a proposed surd square lets you check its rational and irrational parts.",
      "pitfall": "Choose the root consistent with the required positive ratio.",
      "pause": "Find r, then substitute back to find a.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b4-l01",
          "reminder": "Expanding a proposed surd square lets you check its rational and irrational parts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Check and sum the progression",
      "body": "<p>Check that your ratio gives a convergent progression. Then use the sum to infinity and simplify the surd fraction.</p>",
      "recap": "A finite GP sum to infinity requires the absolute ratio to be below 1.",
      "pitfall": "Use r itself in the sum formula, not r squared.",
      "pause": "Evaluate the sum.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l09",
          "reminder": "A finite GP sum to infinity requires the absolute ratio to be below 1."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
